WEBVTT

cue-1
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.720
In this hands-on, we'll create health
checks, monitor key performance signals,

cue-2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.420
and configure an alert that detects
problems before users report them. Let's

cue-3
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.760
begin by identifying how our API should
report its health.

cue-4
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.180
A liveness check answers a simple
question:

cue-5
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.200
Is the application process running and
able to respond?

cue-6
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:32.180
It doesn't check MongoDB because a
database outage shouldn't automatically

cue-7
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.980
restart the Express process.

cue-8
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.160
Readiness answers a different question:

cue-9
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.060
Can this application instance handle real
requests?

cue-10
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.560
For our MERN application, that means
MongoDB must be connected and respond

cue-11
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.020
within an acceptable time.

cue-12
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.840
We'll also monitor latency,

cue-13
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:52.980
traffic,

cue-14
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.480
error rate, and availability.

cue-15
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.960
Together, these signals help us detect
problems that ordinary application logs

cue-16
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.400
might not reveal immediately.

cue-17
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.220
We'll implement the health endpoints using
Claude Code.

cue-18
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.860
Our first endpoint is liveness.

cue-19
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.860
It returns a simple success response
whenever the Express process can answer

cue-20
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.320
requests.

cue-21
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.880
The readiness endpoint performs an
additional check.

cue-22
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.500
It first confirms that Mongoose is
connected, then sends a ping to MongoDB.

cue-23
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.400
We enforce a one-second timeout because a
health check that waits indefinitely

cue-24
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.480
cannot reliably report a problem.

cue-25
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:42.540
When MongoDB responds, readiness returns a
successful status and the measured

cue-26
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.700
database latency.

cue-27
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.860
If the connection fails or the ping takes
too long,

cue-28
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.360
the endpoint returns a 503 response.

cue-29
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.160
Notice that the client receives only a
safe status message.

cue-30
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.500
The detailed failure reason is recorded in
the server logs.

cue-31
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.000
We'll also prevent successful health
checks from filling our logs,

cue-32
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.240
since monitoring systems may call these
endpoints frequently.

cue-33
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.020
Finally,

cue-34
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.720
we'll run the automated tests, including a
simulated database ping that never

cue-35
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.320
responds.

cue-36
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:23.160
This confirms that readiness fails
promptly rather than hanging indefinitely.

cue-37
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.360
Our health checks are working, but we
also need to understand how performance

cue-38
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.000
changes when a dependency becomes slow.

cue-39
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.420
We'll use the prepared performance server
and a small script that calculates

cue-40
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.720
monitoring signals from our structured
JSON logs.

cue-41
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:46.880
First, we'll generate normal API traffic
and measure request throughput, latency

cue-42
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.400
percentiles, and error rate.

cue-43
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.600
These results establish our healthy
baseline.

cue-44
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.340
To simulate a problem, we'll connect to
the local MongoDB instance and enable a

cue-45
00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:57.960
fail point.

cue-46
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.440
The fail point deliberately delays
database commands from our application by

cue-47
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.160
three seconds.

cue-48
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.840
This is strictly a local testing technique
and must never be enabled in production.

cue-49
00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.220
Let's check the health endpoints.

cue-50
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:19.400
Readiness returns 503 because MongoDB
cannot respond within our one-second

cue-51
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:19.740
limit.

cue-52
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.920
Liveness still returns 200 because the
Express process itself remains healthy.

cue-53
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.620
We'll run the load test again and inspect
the performance signals.

cue-54
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.060
Notice how request latency increases
dramatically while throughput falls.

cue-55
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.020
However,

cue-56
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.080
the error rate may remain at zero because
the requests can still complete

cue-57
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.260
successfully, just much more slowly.

cue-58
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.020
This demonstrates why error rate alone
cannot tell us whether users are

cue-59
00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.940
experiencing a healthy application.

cue-60
00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.360
Latency and readiness provide the
additional evidence we need. We've seen

cue-61
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.560
how readiness detects a slow database.

cue-62
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.140
Let's turn that signal into an actionable
alert.

cue-63
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.400
We'll use Claude Code to create a
lightweight monitoring script.

cue-64
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:15.160
The monitor checks the readiness endpoint
every five seconds and records whether the

cue-65
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.320
API is available.

cue-66
00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:22.060
Instead of alerting after one failed
check, we'll require three consecutive

cue-67
00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:22.520
failures.

cue-68
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.480
This helps avoid unnecessary alerts caused
by temporary network issues or brief

cue-69
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.160
interruptions.

cue-70
00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:36.300
We'll also connect the alert to a runbook
containing the steps an on-call developer

cue-71
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:36.880
should follow.

cue-72
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.780
Let's start the monitor while MongoDB is
healthy.

cue-73
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.060
The output confirms that readiness is
passing.

cue-74
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.400
We'll enable the database delay again and
observe the results.

cue-75
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.240
After three consecutive failures, the
monitor prints an alert with the runbook

cue-76
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:54.780
location.

cue-77
00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.680
When we disable the fault, MongoDB
responds normally,

cue-78
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.340
and the monitor reports that the incident
has resolved.

cue-79
00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.600
The same monitoring principle can be
configured on a production hosting

cue-80
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:14.140
platform or external uptime service. We've
implemented separate liveness and

cue-81
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.000
readiness checks,

cue-82
00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.040
monitored key API performance signals,

cue-83
00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.860
and created an alert that detects repeated
readiness failures.

cue-84
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:30.480
Thank you for watching, and I will see you
in the next video. 
